-- Q2442: Tax Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 6212 and 6213, the IRS issues a 30-day letter proposing adjustments, then the taxpayer may appeal to the IRS Appeals Office. If unresolved, the IRS issues a statutory notice of deficiency (90-day letter), and the taxpayer may petition Tax Court within 90 days for pre-payment adjudication.
Wrong (A): The 90-day letter does not precede the 30-day letter; the 30-day letter is always issued first.
Wrong (B): Appeals comes after the 30-day letter, not before it, and District Court requires prior payment of the deficiency.
Wrong (C): A Tax Court petition follows the 90-day letter, not the 30-day letter; this reverses the correct sequence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2442;

-- Q2444: Tax Procedures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 6654(d)(1)(B), taxpayers avoid the estimated tax penalty by paying at least 90% of current-year tax or 100% of prior-year tax. For taxpayers with prior-year AGI exceeding $150,000, the prior-year safe harbor increases to 110% under Section 6654(d)(1)(C). Henderson''s AGI of $130,000 qualifies for the standard 100% threshold.
Wrong (A): The 80% threshold is not recognized under IRC Sec. 6654; the current-year safe harbor is 90%.
Wrong (B): Paying 100% of current-year tax would avoid the penalty, but this choice ignores the alternative prior-year safe harbor.
Wrong (D): The 50% threshold has no basis in the estimated tax penalty provisions of the IRC.$EXPL$
