-- Q2459: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under common law and UCC Section 2-210, contract rights—especially the right to receive payment—are freely assignable unless the contract expressly prohibits assignment or it would materially change the obligor''s duty. Davis''s assignment of payment rights to First Bank is valid.
Wrong (B): Evans''s consent is not required for assignment of payment rights; only the duty to perform personal services cannot be delegated without consent.
Wrong (C): The right to receive payment is not a personal service obligation; only Davis''s duty to paint would resist delegation.
Wrong (D): First Bank receives the right to payment, not the duty to perform; the assignee of a payment right has no obligation to render services.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2459;

-- Q2460: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-601, the perfect tender rule requires exact conformity in sale-of-goods contracts. Delivering only 80 of 100 contracted widgets is a material breach entitling Harper to reject the nonconforming goods, suspend performance, and sue for damages.
Wrong (A): A 20% shortfall under the UCC perfect tender rule is not a minor breach; any nonconformity permits rejection.
Wrong (B): Substantial performance is a common law doctrine that does not apply to UCC sale-of-goods contracts governed by perfect tender.
Wrong (C): Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party communicates intent not to perform before the due date; Garcia did perform, just insufficiently.$EXPL$
