  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-201(2), between merchants, a written confirmation sufficient against the sender satisfies the Statute of Frauds against the recipient if the recipient does not object in writing within 10 days. Baxter''s silence binds it as if it had signed the confirmation.
Wrong (B): The merchant exception under Section 2-201(2) eliminates the requirement for the recipient''s own signature when the recipient fails to object within 10 days.
Wrong (C): The 10-day objection window is absolute; a merchant cannot object months or years later under the general statute of limitations.
Wrong (D): There is no penalty for failing to respond; the consequence is that the confirmation satisfies the Statute of Frauds against the recipient.$EXPL$
