-- Q2480: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 1.01, an agency relationship is created when one person (the agent) agrees to act on behalf of another (the principal), subject to the principal''s control. Mutual consent, benefit to the principal, and the right of control are essential elements.
Wrong (B): A partnership agreement creates co-owners, not an agency relationship; partnerships are governed by separate statutes.
Wrong (C): An employee hired with no supervision lacks the control element essential to agency; the principal must retain the right to direct the agent.
Wrong (D): A purchase transaction is a buyer-seller relationship, not an agency relationship involving acting on another''s behalf.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2480;

-- Q2481: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.01, express authority arises when the principal makes an explicit communication—oral or written—granting the agent specific authority to act. Caldwell''s written agreement directly confers express authority on Fletcher.
Wrong (A): Authority assumed from circumstances describes implied authority under Section 2.02, not express authority.
Wrong (B): A third party''s reasonable belief describes apparent authority under Section 2.03, not express authority.
Wrong (C): Emergency action to protect the principal''s interests describes authority by necessity, not express authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2481;

-- Q2482: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.02, implied authority includes the power to perform acts reasonably necessary, incidental to, or customary for carrying out the agent''s express authority. Arranging shipping as a purchasing agent is customary for that role.
Wrong (A): Implied authority does not extend to any action the agent believes beneficial; it is limited to acts reasonably connected to the express authorization.
Wrong (C): Implied authority does not permit exceeding the scope of express authorization; it covers only incidental and customary acts.
Wrong (D): An agent generally cannot delegate all responsibilities to a subagent without the principal''s consent unless delegation is customary for the role.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2482;

-- Q2483: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.03, apparent authority exists when the principal''s conduct—such as granting a title, providing business cards, and allowing trade show representation—causes a reasonable third party to believe the agent has authority to act.
Wrong (A): Actual authority not communicated to third parties is simply undisclosed actual authority, not apparent authority.
Wrong (B): An agent claiming authority without any basis from the principal''s conduct does not create apparent authority; the principal must cause the appearance.
Wrong (C): Exceeding express authority in an emergency describes authority by necessity, not apparent authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2483;

-- Q2485: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.01, a gratuitous agent owes the same fiduciary duties as a compensated agent, including loyalty, obedience, care, accounting, and notification. Accepting the agency relationship—not compensation—triggers these obligations.
Wrong (A): The absence of consideration does not eliminate fiduciary duties; the duties arise from the agency relationship itself.
Wrong (B): A gratuitous agent owes both the duty of loyalty and the duty of care, not just one of the two.
Wrong (D): Fiduciary duties are imposed by law upon accepting an agency relationship and are not limited to what is expressly stated in writing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2485;

-- Q2486: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.04, respondeat superior holds an employer vicariously liable for torts committed by an employee acting within the scope of employment. Liability attaches even if the employer did not authorize or know about the specific tortious act.
Wrong (A): Liability is limited to acts within the scope of employment; personal errands or frolics and detours generally do not trigger employer liability.
Wrong (B): Respondeat superior applies to torts (negligence, etc.), not criminal acts; employers are generally not vicariously liable for employee crimes.
Wrong (C): Respondeat superior covers tort liability, not just contractual obligations; it is distinct from contractual agency principles.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2486;

-- Q2487: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 6.01, when an agent acts for a disclosed principal, the principal is the real party in interest. The third party''s remedies run against the principal, and the agent drops out of the transaction unless the agent personally guaranteed performance.
Wrong (A): The agent is not personally liable for signing on behalf of a disclosed principal; liability falls on the principal.
Wrong (B): Joint liability of both agent and principal does not apply when the principal is disclosed; the principal alone is bound.
Wrong (D): The third party does not assume the risk; the disclosed principal is contractually bound and subject to remedies for nonperformance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2487;

-- Q2488: Agency and Regulation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 6.03, when an agent acts for an undisclosed principal, the third party may hold either the agent or the principal liable once the principal''s identity is discovered. The agent remains liable because the third party relied on the agent''s personal credit.
Wrong (A): The third party does have recourse; once the undisclosed principal''s identity is discovered, the third party may pursue remedies against either party.
Wrong (B): Both the agent and the undisclosed principal are potentially liable, not just the agent; the third party must generally elect one.
Wrong (D): The contract is not automatically void; it remains valid and enforceable against both the agent and the undisclosed principal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2488;

